            "Community health nursing treats the <strong>family</strong> — the group that shares a home, resources, obligations and a history, by blood, marriage or choice — as the unit of care, because the family is where health is produced and lost: who eats first and who eats last, who fetches the water and who decides to spend the money on the clinic, who sleeps under the net, who notices the cough and who forbids the injection. In Ghana the <strong>nuclear</strong> household of parents and children sits inside an <strong>extended</strong> family whose head, elders and 'abusuapanyin' hold authority over marriage, land, funerals and often over illness; <strong>polygamous</strong> households share a husband between wives whose children compete for his resources; <strong>single-parent</strong> families, usually headed by women, are common and poorer; <strong>child-headed</strong> and <strong>grandparent-headed</strong> ('skipped-generation') households are left by HIV, migration and the kayayei trade; and <strong>migrant</strong> families, blended families and cohabiting couples complete the picture. The average household has between three and four people, but a compound house in Kumasi may hold forty. <strong>Matrilineal</strong> peoples — the Akan — trace descent and inheritance through the mother's line, so that a child belongs to his mother's family and his mother's brother carries responsibilities his father may not; <strong>patrilineal</strong> peoples — the Ewe, Ga-Dangme and most northern groups — trace through the father. The Intestate Succession Law, 1985 protects the widow and children of a man who dies without a will, but custom still strips many of them; the nurse who understands descent knows whom to ask about the sick child, whom to involve in the family planning decision, and who will inherit the responsibility for the widow.",
