            "<strong>Assessment</strong> begins with structure. The <strong>genogram</strong> draws three generations — squares for men, circles for women, a line for a partnership, children below, the index person shaded, deaths crossed — and shows at a glance the sickle cell, hypertension, diabetes, twins and early deaths that run through the family, the polygamous households, the absent fathers and the grandmother raising five. The <strong>eco-map</strong> draws the family in the centre and the people and institutions around it — church or mosque, the clinic, the school, the employer, the landlord, the sister in Accra, the susu group, the healer — with thick lines for strong support, thin or dashed for weak, and jagged lines for relationships that drain, and it answers who will actually help. The <strong>family APGAR</strong> asks five quick questions about Adaptation, Partnership, Growth, Affection and Resolve to score how the family functions. The assessment continues with the <strong>home environment</strong> (water, latrine, refuse, cooking, sleeping arrangements, nets, animals, hazards), the <strong>health practices and beliefs</strong> (where they go first when ill, what they do with fever, who delivers the babies, what the children eat), the <strong>health status</strong> of each member (the register's immunisation, pregnancy, chronic disease and nutrition data), the family's <strong>resources</strong> (income, NHIS cards, land, education, relatives who send money) and its <strong>strengths</strong> — the mother who always brings the children, the father who reads, the grandmother who is respected.",
