            "The <strong>home visit</strong> is the defining activity of the community health nurse and the only place where the cause of a recurring illness is visible: the clinic shows the diarrhoea, the house shows the uncovered water pot. Its <strong>purposes</strong> are to assess the family and its environment, to give care that cannot wait for the clinic (the postnatal mother and newborn, the bedridden, the person with severe malnutrition on outpatient treatment), to teach in the place where the behaviour happens, to follow up and trace defaulters, to find the unregistered pregnant woman and the unimmunised child, to conduct surveillance, to support the carer and to build the relationship that makes the family come when it matters. Its <strong>principles</strong>: it is planned and purposeful, never a social call without an aim; it is based on need and priority; it is at a time convenient to the family and agreed in advance where possible; it is conducted with respect, acceptance and without judgement of the household's poverty or beliefs; it keeps confidentiality — a visit to the house of a person with HIV or tuberculosis must look like every other visit; it involves the family in every decision; it uses the family's own resources; it is educational; it is recorded; and it protects the nurse's safety.",
