            "About 8 per cent of Ghanaians aged five and over report at least some difficulty seeing, hearing, walking, remembering, caring for themselves or communicating — visual impairment the commonest, then physical, hearing, intellectual, speech and psychosocial disability, and albinism with its own needs. The causes are the subject of the rest of this course: birth asphyxia and kernicterus (cerebral palsy), meningitis and measles, untreated ear infection, cataract and glaucoma, onchocerciasis, trachoma and leprosy in older people, Buruli ulcer contractures, injuries on the road and at work, stroke, diabetic amputation and untreated epilepsy and mental illness — and most are preventable by the immunisation, the clean birth, the early treatment and the safe road described in earlier lessons. <strong>Stigma</strong> hides disabled children at home and out of school, labels the child with cerebral palsy a 'spirit child' to be killed in some northern communities, chains the person with a mental disability, puts the blind man on the street to beg, and denies disabled women contraception, maternity care and protection from the sexual violence to which they are more exposed than any other group.",
